Some scenarios require server-side file processing — virus scanning before storage, image resizing, or watermarking. In these cases, stream the file instead of buffering it entirely in memory.

The AWS SDK v2 `S3Client` supports both approaches. For uploads, `RequestBody.fromInputStream()` streams directly from the multipart request. For downloads, `ResponseInputStream` lets you process data in chunks without loading the full object into heap.

```java
@Service
public class FileStorageService {
    private final S3Client s3;
    private static final String BUCKET = "my-app-uploads";

    public FileStorageService(S3Client s3) {
        this.s3 = s3;
    }

    public String upload(String key, InputStream content,
                         long contentLength, String contentType) {
        s3.putObject(
            PutObjectRequest.builder()
                .bucket(BUCKET).key(key)
                .contentType(contentType)
                .contentLength(contentLength) // required for streaming
                .build(),
            RequestBody.fromInputStream(content, contentLength));
        return "s3://" + BUCKET + "/" + key;
    }

    public void download(String key, OutputStream target) throws IOException {
        try (var response = s3.getObject(
                GetObjectRequest.builder().bucket(BUCKET).key(key).build())) {
            response.transferTo(target); // streams without full heap load
        }
    }
}
```

The `contentLength` parameter on upload is required for streaming — without it, the SDK buffers the entire stream into memory to calculate the size, defeating the purpose of streaming. If you don't know the content length upfront, use the S3 multipart upload API which uploads in chunks.

Presigned URLs are the highest-impact pattern for file-heavy applications. Your Spring Boot server generates a URL that's signed with your IAM credentials and valid for a limited time. The client uses this URL to communicate directly with S3 — your server is never in the data path.

The `S3Presigner` (SDK v2) generates both download (GET) and upload (PUT) URLs. The expiry time is a security decision: long enough for the transfer, short enough that a leaked URL doesn't provide indefinite access.

```java
@Service
public class PresignedUrlService {
    private final S3Presigner presigner;
    private static final String BUCKET = "my-app-uploads";

    public PresignedUrlService(S3Presigner presigner) {
        this.presigner = presigner;
    }

    public URL generateUploadUrl(String key, String contentType, int expiryMinutes) {
        var request = PutObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(BUCKET).key(key)
            .contentType(contentType)
            .build();
        return presigner.presignPutObject(b -> b
            .signatureDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(expiryMinutes))
            .putObjectRequest(request))
            .url();
    }

    public URL generateDownloadUrl(String key, int expiryMinutes) {
        var request = GetObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(BUCKET).key(key)
            .build();
        return presigner.presignGetObject(b -> b
            .signatureDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(expiryMinutes))
            .getObjectRequest(request))
            .url();
    }
}
```

The client uploads with a simple `PUT` request to the presigned URL — no AWS SDK needed on the client side. This works from any HTTP client, including browser `fetch()`. The `contentType` must match what was specified during URL generation, or S3 rejects the upload with a `SignatureDoesNotMatch` error.

For downloads, serve the presigned URL as a redirect (`302`) from your API endpoint. The client follows the redirect and downloads directly from S3's CDN-grade infrastructure — faster than proxying and zero load on your server.
